\section{A Finite Space of Possible Facts}

A compiler can expose its control-flow graph as relations instead of asking an engine to manipulate syntax trees. Blocks become symbols, edges become rows, and an analysis states how to derive more rows. In the positive Datalog fragment used here, terms are only variables and constants. No function symbols can build arbitrary compound terms, and no operation generates new constants. Predicates have fixed arities. All head variables must occur in a positive body atom; facts are ground. These restrictions give a finite ground language for a finite input and program.

The \emph{Herbrand base} \(H\) is the collection of ground atoms obtained by putting program and input constants into every predicate's argument positions. With \(n\) constants, a binary predicate has at most \(n^2\) possible tuples, regardless of the number of proofs for each tuple. The base is a bound, not a prescription to allocate every tuple. Grounding a rule means replacing its variables by constants consistently: all occurrences of one variable get the same constant. Operational tricks from Prolog, such as constructing \verb|next(next(Block))| as a term or using a cut to pick an answer, are not part of this fragment.

\section{Reachability as a Souffl\'e Program}

Here is a complete example in Souffl\'e syntax \cite{jordan2016souffle}. The fixed input relations (the extensional database) are \texttt{start} and \texttt{edge}; \texttt{live} is an intensional relation defined by rules. Here ``live'' means reachable, not variable liveness. The declarations give column types; they do not prescribe the order in which a rule should read them.

\begin{verbatim}
.decl start(b:symbol)
.decl edge(src:symbol, dst:symbol)
.decl live(b:symbol)
.output live

start("entry").
edge("entry", "test").
edge("test", "body").
edge("test", "exit").
edge("body", "test").
edge("dead", "exit").

live(b) :- start(b).
live(y) :- live(x), edge(x, y).
\end{verbatim}

The result contains \texttt{entry}, \texttt{test}, \texttt{body}, and \texttt{exit}, with no promised row order. An edge out of \texttt{dead} does not make its source reachable, and the cycle does not create new copies of existing facts. The same program rules are applicable to every input graph of this shape; replacing the input facts does not require editing the rules.

\section{The Immediate Consequence Operator}

Let \(E\) be the input facts. For \(I\subseteq H\), let \(T_P(I)\) be \(E\) together with all heads of ground rule instances whose bodies are contained in \(I\). Positive body tests persist as \(I\) grows, so \(T_P\) is monotone under set inclusion. A set \(M\) is a model of the program over this base if \(T_P(M)\subseteq M\): it contains the input and is closed under the rules. Thus a model may contain arbitrary extra tuples, but the \emph{least} model contains exactly the ones forced by the facts and rules. Van Emden and Kowalski's \emph{The Semantics of Predicate Logic as a Programming Language} establishes the connection between fixed points and least Herbrand models for definite clauses; the finite case here permits termination by explicit saturation. Abiteboul, Hull, and Vianu develop this database interpretation \cite{abiteboul1995foundations}.

