\section{Walkthrough: Saturate from the Given Facts}

The following is specification pseudocode, not an efficient grounding algorithm. The snapshot makes the notion of a round precise.

\begin{verbatim}
known := E
repeat:
    snapshot := known
    proposed := empty set
    for each ground rule (head :- body):
        if body is a subset of snapshot:
            proposed.add(head)           # visible next round
    known := snapshot union proposed
until known equals snapshot
\end{verbatim}

The invariant is that \texttt{known} is a subset of every model containing \(E\), since a newly inserted tuple follows from facts already forced in every model. The first round adds \texttt{entry}, the second adds \texttt{test}, and the third adds \texttt{body} and \texttt{exit}. In the fourth round the back edge can only rederive \texttt{test}. At equality the result is closed and hence is itself a model; the invariant makes it the least one. Every strict round adds at least one ground atom from \(H\), so only finitely many strict rounds occur. This is also the least fixed point of \(T_P\): at convergence each stored fact is either input or had a supporting rule in an earlier round, and that rule still fires at convergence. \Cref{fig:visual-343} shows the round in which each reachable block first enters the result.

