This example is a may-analysis: one path is enough. Its growth by union is a special case of the monotone fixed-point analyses treated by Khedker, Sanyal, and Karkare in \emph{Data Flow Analysis: Theory and Practice}. A must-property requires accounting for all relevant paths, not just swapping an edge direction. Unlike a Prolog goal trace, the semantic rounds describe sets of derived facts, not the order in which a caller sees answers. A bottom-up engine can change its evaluation schedule and still compute the same positive least model.

For compiler construction, publish the saturated relation together with the CFG snapshot from which it was derived. A later optimization may rely on membership in \texttt{live}, but must not combine rows from an old graph with rows from a newly extracted one.
